at the core of the book.\n\nVolume 28 Issue 2 June 2020\n\nJournal Article\n\n# Persi Diaconis and Brian Skyrms. Ten Great Ideas about Chance\n\nGet access\n\nPersi Diaconis and Brian Skyrms. Ten Great Ideas about Chance\n\n.\n\nPrinceton University Press\n\n,\n\n2017\n\n. Pp. xiv + 255. ISBN: 978-0-691-17416-7 (hbk); 978-0-691-19639-8 (pbk), 978-1-40088828-3 (e-book).\n\nChristian Hennig\n\nChristian Hennig\n\nDepartment of Statistical Sciences ‘Paolo Fortunati’\n\n, University of Bologna, 40126 Bologna,\n\nItaly\n\nOrcid.org/0000-0003-1550-5637. E-mail: [email protected]\n\nSearch for other works by this author on:\n\nOxford Academic\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\nPhilosophia Mathematica, Volume 28, Issue 2, June 2020, Pages 282–285, https://doi.org/10.1093/philmat/nkaa018\n\nPublished:\n\n22 April 2020\n\n## Extract\n\nIn the preface, Diaconis and Skyrms call this book ‘a history book, a probability book, and a philosophy book’. The book is based on a course that the authors taught at Stanford on the foundations of probability for an apparently quite interdisciplinary and also mixed-age audience. As a prerequisite ‘one course in probability or statistics’ was required for the course. The book has a probability refresher for those who took such a course very long ago.\n\nThe book has ten chapters, each of them devoted to one ‘great idea’, and the exploration of these ideas, not necessarily always in chronological order, is at the core of the book. Here they are:\n\nChapter 1 is on ‘Measurement’. This treats the beginnings of measuring probability numerically, based on counting equiprobable cases, from Cardano to Bernoulli’s law of large numbers. The chapter has a nice discussion on the physics of coin flipping, and how this could become the archetypical example for a random process despite not actually being ‘objectively random’.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n8. Algorithmic Randomness (https://www.degruyterbrill.com/docum